=================================================================================
THE DESIGN
=================================================================================
A trigger has three observable consequences, and this case measures all three, each with a
control that must NOT produce it (STANDING-ORDERS §7):
  (a) its own counter -- `show trigger N` "Number of activations" and "Last activation";
  (b) an SNMP trap on the wire, if the trigger carries `trap` -- captured with tcpdump on
      tb277 eth3 and decoded with `tools/snmpwire.py`.  This is the frame-level evidence the
      standing orders require; `show trigger` alone would be the `show`-command trap that
      `platforms/x960.md` §5 warns about;
  (c) the DUT's own `TRIGGER[...]` log lines -- a third, independent instrument.

TWO triggers were built deliberately, so that each event discriminates between them:
      trigger 1 = `type interface port1.0.2 down`
      trigger 2 = `type interface port1.0.2 up`
One `shutdown` must fire 1 and only 1; one `no shutdown` must fire 2 and only 2.

The port is **port1.0.2**, as the dispatch requires.  port1.0.10 and port1.0.48 carry the only
IP path from the box to the DUT -- which IS the instrument here, since the trap has to reach
10.38.22.33 -- and were never touched.  port1.0.16 is the sibling control.

SETUP APPLIED BY THIS CASE (before step 1) -- the trap instrument only
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
    awplus# configure terminal
    awplus(config)# snmp-server community testing123 ro
    awplus(config)# snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123
    awplus(config)# end
    awplus# show running-config | include snmp
    snmp-server
    snmp-server community testing123
    snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version 2c testing123

The triggers themselves are NOT part of setup: creating them is the subject under test and is
STEP 2, so that STEP 1 can be a genuine baseline.  Note there is no `snmp-server enable trap`
line: `snmp-server enable trap ?` offers 33 groups (atmf, auth, bgp, ... rmon, nsm, vrrp ...)
and **none of them is a trigger group** -- the per-trigger `trap` command is self-contained.
That was measured, not assumed (work/w-trapgroups.log), and it is why the baseline in STEP 1 is
silent even with a trap host configured.
All secrets are the campaign's worthless `testing123`.

