=================================================================================
NOT MEASURED, SAID PLAINLY
=================================================================================
* **THE SCRIPT'S CONTENT WAS NOT RUN.**  A trigger's action is a `.scp` file on the device,
  there is none on this DUT's flash (`dir flash:` above), and putting one there needs a file
  the DUT can fetch: the supported route is the box's `/tftproot` plus
  `copy tftp://<box>/<file> flash:/<name>` (memory `file-to-unit-via-device-copy-tftp`), and
  writing to `/tftproot` is a ROOT write on the box -- STANDING-ORDERS §4, never done
  unattended.  This is the SAME open limb T44181 left on 2026-10-16 and it is item 5 on the
  2026-10-19 session handover's OPEN list; this case does not close it.  What this case DID
  add is that the trigger demonstrably reaches the point of opening its configured script
  (STEP 6), so the gap is now narrowed to "what a script does when it runs", not "does the
  trigger run its action".
* **REBOOT PERSISTENCE IS NOT MEASURED**, for this case and the whole group.  The DUT's
  underlay exists in the RUNNING configuration only and the boot config is
  `flash:/default.cfg`; a reload would destroy the path every case in this campaign measures
  through, and writing the underlay to startup-config is a bench change (STANDING-ORDERS §4)
  not made unattended.  So this case does NOT show that a trigger survives a reboot.  Same
  declared gap as groups A and B.
* `type interface port1.0.2 any` (the third keyword) and the bare `<cr>` form were NOT
  exercised; `down` and `up` were, which is what discriminates.  `day`, `time`, `repeat <n>`,
  `repeat once` and `test` mode were not exercised either -- enumerated in STEP 0, not graded.
* The trigger was never bound to an interface that is NOT physically cabled, so "an admin-down
  on a dark port" is not covered.

